window.docContents[1].resolve({"/Elementary-Functions/___4___37-Inverse-Hyperbolic-Functions/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___37-Inverse-Hyperbolic-Functions--___4___37-Inverse-Hyperbolic-Functions--General-definitions":{"contents":"Source: DLMF §4.37(i)\n\nE1–E6 are the path-integral definitions\nArcsinh z=∫₀ᶻdt/√(1+t²), Arccosh z=∫₁ᶻdt/√(t²−1),\nArctanh z=∫₀ᶻdt/(1−t²), and the reciprocal identities for Arccsch,\nArcsech, and Arccoth. Paths avoid ±i for the sinh pair and ±1 otherwise.\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Elementary Functions\u0009§4.37 Inverse Hyperbolic Functions\u0009§4.37 Inverse Hyperbolic Functions","header":"General definitions","id":"/Elementary-Functions/___4___37-Inverse-Hyperbolic-Functions/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___37-Inverse-Hyperbolic-Functions--___4___37-Inverse-Hyperbolic-Functions--General-definitions"},"/Elementary-Functions/___4___48-Software/#chapter-4-section-48":{"contents":"Source: NIST Digital Library of Mathematical Functions, §4.48.\n\n\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Elementary Functions","header":"4.48. §4.48 Software","id":"/Elementary-Functions/___4___48-Software/#chapter-4-section-48"},"/Exponential___-Logarithmic___-Sine___-and-Cosine-Integrals/___6___18-Methods-of-Computation/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Exponential___-Logarithmic___-Sine___-and-Cosine-Integrals--___6___18-Methods-of-Computation--___6___18-Methods-of-Computation--Auxiliary-functions":{"contents":"Power series (§6.6), asymptotic expansions\n(§6.12), and quadrature (see\n§6.14) also compute the auxiliary functions\nf and g. Acton (1974) gives the recurrence procedure used here. For\nn=0,1,2,\\dots, the three integrals are represented on the right half-plane\nRe z>0; the Lean RightHalfPlane subtype packages this convergence\nhypothesis once, so the canonical maps below do not repeat a proof argument.\n\nThe displayed values are the ordinary Bochner integrals over the positive\nray. Their convergence as improper integrals is recorded separately, so the\ndefinition remains a direct mathematical object while the convergence claim is\navailable to later theorems.\n\n\n\nA_n=\\int_0^\\infty\\frac{te^{-zt}}{1+t^2}\\left(\\frac{t^2}{1+t^2}\\right)^n dt,\\quad B_n=\\int_0^\\infty\\frac{e^{-zt}}{1+t^2}\\left(\\frac{t^2}{1+t^2}\\right)^n dt,\\quad C_n=\\int_0^\\infty e^{-zt}\\left(\\frac{t^2}{1+t^2}\\right)^n dt$$\n\nThus the base auxiliary values are f(z)=B₀(z) and g(z)=A₀(z). For\nn=1,2,3,\\dots, integration identities give the downward recurrence:\n\nA_{n-1}=A_n+\\frac{z}{2n}C_n,\\quad B_{n-1}=\\frac{2nB_n+zA_{n-1}}{2n-1},\\quad C_{n-1}=C_n+B_{n-1}$$\n\nThe initial values A₀, B₀, and C₀ can be computed by Miller's\nbackward algorithm, as described in §3.6(iii):\nstart at an arbitrary large index with (A_N,B_N,C_N)=(1,0,0) and normalize\nby C₀=1/z.\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Exponential, Logarithmic, Sine, and Cosine Integrals\u0009§6.18 Methods of Computation\u0009§6.18 Methods of Computation","header":"Auxiliary functions","id":"/Exponential___-Logarithmic___-Sine___-and-Cosine-Integrals/___6___18-Methods-of-Computation/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Exponential___-Logarithmic___-Sine___-and-Cosine-Integrals--___6___18-Methods-of-Computation--___6___18-Methods-of-Computation--Auxiliary-functions"}});